        </relations>
    </header>
    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="27" relname="span">My thoughts are that how intuitive an interface might be is based entirely on its audience.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="28" relname="span">Individual differences can be grouped into four categories:</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="29" relname="list">(1) knowledge, experience, and skill;</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="29" relname="list">(2) personality factors;</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="29" relname="list">(3) physical and demographic attributes;</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="29" relname="list">and (4) user levels (Badre et al, 2002).</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="27" relname="elaboration">A working definition of intuitiveness could be the probability that a defined inexperienced user can intuitively figure out how to do a defined set of tasks correctly (ConfirmIt, 2004).</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="31" relname="span">To measure intuitiveness, we would have different levels of interfaces:</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="41" relname="list">General users (mp3 players, DVD players, e-mail programs)</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="41" relname="list">Sophisticated Users (video games, mp3 ripping, paint programs)</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="41" relname="list">Professional Users (Photoshop, accounting, music recording)</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="41" relname="list">Extreme Professionals (AutoCad, video game developers, programming languages)</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="32" relname="nonvolitional-cause">Since everyone brings different levels of intelligence and historical knowledge with them to the table,</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="33" relname="span">it would be best to determine how intuitive an interface is by using sample groups of users of various skill levels.</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="39" relname="list">Everyone who uses a general user type program should be able to figure it out with minimum effort.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="39" relname="list">Also, most of the architects in a sample group should be able to do the basic functions of a new CAD application.</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="39" relname="nonvolitional-result">When these fail, then the interface could be deemed (gasp) unintuitive.</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="40" relname="span">One could set up matrices that say:</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="35" relname="span">Tasks A, and B are very simple everyone should be able to do this.</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="19" relname="elaboration">Ex: Start and Stop the CD-player.</segment>
        <segment id="21" parent="37" relname="concession">Task C is a little harder,</segment>
        <segment id="22" parent="37" relname="span">but assuming a good intuitive interface, everyone should be able to do this, too.</segment>
        <segment id="23" parent="22" relname="elaboration">Ex: Eject the CD or get a play list.</segment>
        <segment id="24" parent="34" relname="list">And so on.</segment>
